00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:27.030
Anubhav Kokane: now let's move to…

186
00:34:28.449 --> 00:34:39.429
Anubhav Kokane: Token storage. So, token storage is also a crucial topic. The refresh tokens are stored in HTTP-only secure cookies, making them inaccessible to JavaScript.

187
00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:44.910
Anubhav Kokane: So, this helps protect against XSS-based tokens, theft, theft.

188
00:34:45.139 --> 00:34:57.160
Anubhav Kokane: Now, the access tokens are short-lived, so they are stored directly in client's memory. Since they expire quickly and are not persisted anywhere else, this significantly reduces the

189
00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:09.260
Anubhav Kokane: Wind-off exposure. Now, if required, we can also utilize WebWorkers and IndexDB for Additional isolation.

190
00:35:09.790 --> 00:35:16.919
Anubhav Kokane: We make sure that the tokens are not stored in local storage, making them vulnerable to token theft.

191
00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:23.320
Anubhav Kokane: Moving to, token validation and a refresh flow.

192
00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:34.729
Anubhav Kokane: So, all the token… all the API requests made with tokens are validated on the server. We verify the token signature, check the expiration, and also.

193
00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:36.820
Anubhav Kokane: Confirm the issuer.

194
00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:43.989
Anubhav Kokane: Now, whenever our access token is expired.

195
00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:53.699
Anubhav Kokane: It is… we use refresh tokens to generate new access tokens without needing to log in again.

196
00:35:54.200 --> 00:36:04.199
Anubhav Kokane: Also, whenever a refresh token is used to generate a new access token, the old refresh token is invalidated, and a new one is created.

197
00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:07.010
Anubhav Kokane: This helps.

198
00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:10.399
Anubhav Kokane: Prevent any token replay attacks.

199
00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:18.550
Anubhav Kokane: Now… Moving to other security protocols and token revocation.

200
00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:24.190
Anubhav Kokane: So, if any token is compromised, they are blacklisted, and

201
00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.209
Anubhav Kokane: To immediately revoke the access.

202
00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:32.930
Anubhav Kokane: We have also enforced rate limiting, so to prevent brute force and

203
00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:43.240
Anubhav Kokane: DOS attacks. Also, a strict content security policy has been defined, which whitelists all the trusted sources.

204
00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:46.000
Anubhav Kokane: To prevent data injection.

205
00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:47.780
Anubhav Kokane: attacks.

